<LegalLayout
  title="HIPAA Notice — Implenix"
  heading="HIPAA Notice"
  description="How Implenix, as a HIPAA business associate, safeguards protected health information: BAAs with every client, the minimum-necessary standard, and administrative, physical, and technical safeguards."
>
  <p>
    Implenix provides medical billing and revenue cycle management services to
    healthcare practices. In doing so, we act as a <strong>business
    associate</strong> as defined by the Health Insurance Portability and
    Accountability Act of 1996 ("HIPAA") and its implementing regulations,
    including the Privacy Rule, Security Rule, and Breach Notification Rule.
    This notice explains how we approach our HIPAA obligations. It is provided
    for transparency and does not replace the Business Associate Agreement we
    sign with each client.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Business Associate Agreements</h2>
  <p>
    We sign a <strong>Business Associate Agreement (BAA)</strong> with every
    client before any protected health information ("PHI") is exchanged — no
    exceptions. Our BAA obligates us to:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use and disclose PHI only as permitted by the BAA and as required to
      perform the billing services engaged;</li>
    <li>Implement the safeguards required by the HIPAA Security Rule;</li>
    <li>Report security incidents and breaches of unsecured PHI to the client
      without unreasonable delay;</li>
    <li>Ensure any subcontractor that handles PHI on our behalf agrees in
      writing to the same restrictions and conditions;</li>
    <li>Make PHI available for access, amendment, and accounting of
      disclosures as required by the Privacy Rule;</li>
    <li>Return or destroy PHI at the end of the engagement where feasible, as
      directed by the client.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If your practice has its own BAA template, we are glad to review it; we
    can also provide ours.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Minimum-necessary standard</h2>
  <p>
    We apply the <strong>minimum-necessary standard</strong> to every use and
    disclosure of PHI. Our staff access only the information required to
    perform the specific billing task at hand — for example, the claim data
    needed to submit, correct, appeal, or follow up on a claim. Access to
    client systems is requested with the narrowest scope that still allows us
    to do the work, and access rights are reviewed and revoked when roles
    change or engagements end.
  </p>

  <h2>3. How we safeguard PHI</h2>
  <h3>Administrative safeguards</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Documented HIPAA policies and procedures, reviewed regularly;</li>
    <li>Workforce HIPAA training at hire and periodically thereafter;</li>
    <li>Confidentiality agreements for all workforce members;</li>
    <li>A designated security official responsible for our HIPAA program;</li>
    <li>Periodic risk assessments and remediation of identified gaps;</li>
    <li>A documented incident response and breach notification process.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Technical safeguards</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Encryption of PHI in transit (TLS) and at rest;</li>
    <li>Unique user accounts, strong authentication, and role-based access
      controls — no shared logins;</li>
    <li>Automatic session timeouts and device screen locks;</li>
    <li>Audit logging of access to systems that store or process PHI;</li>
    <li>No PHI in unencrypted email or consumer messaging apps.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Physical safeguards</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Controlled access to work areas and devices used for client work;</li>
    <li>Encrypted, managed devices for workforce members;</li>
    <li>Secure disposal of any physical media containing PHI.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Uses and disclosures of PHI</h2>
  <p>As a business associate, we use and disclose PHI only:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>To perform the billing and revenue cycle services set out in our
      agreement with the client — for example, submitting claims to payers,
      responding to payer requests, pursuing appeals, and preparing patient
      statements;</li>
    <li>As required by law;</li>
    <li>For our own proper management and administration, where permitted by
      the BAA and HIPAA.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We never sell PHI, and we never use PHI for marketing.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Breach notification</h2>
  <p>
    In the event of a breach of unsecured PHI, we will notify the affected
    client without unreasonable delay and within the timeframe set by our BAA,
    and we will cooperate fully with the client's obligations under the Breach
    Notification Rule, including providing the information the client needs to
    notify affected individuals and regulators.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Patients: questions about your health information</h2>
  <p>
    If you are a patient and have received a bill or statement in connection
    with services we provide to your healthcare provider, your provider's
    Notice of Privacy Practices governs how your health information is used.
    Requests to access, amend, or restrict your health information should be
    directed to your provider. If you contact us instead, we will route your
    request to the right place.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Contact our privacy team</h2>
  <p>
    For BAA requests, security questionnaires, or any question about our HIPAA
    program:
  </p>
  <address>
    <strong>Implenix — Privacy &amp; Security</strong><br />
    1879 NW 8th St, Miami, FL 33125<br />
    <a href="mailto:contact@implenix.net">contact@implenix.net</a>
  </address>
</LegalLayout>
